Chapter: TlsFetchLoopbackTest
  cites Net chapter TlsEndpoint
  cites Encode chapter X509
  cites Encode chapter X509Chain
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 An AUTHENTICATED TLS 1.3 handshake over a reliable stream, end to end,
 with no network -- and then application data carried both ways under the
 application traffic keys. This is what BACKLOG 1.9 needs: a client that
 speaks real TLS to a server it can authenticate, so an https fetch is no
 longer a refusal.

 The server presents the same real Ed25519 leaf certificate the DTLS
 loopback uses (minted outside Codex; signatures by our RFC-8032-gated
 Ed25519). The client carries the CA as its trust anchor and REQUIRES
 authentication. The negative at the end proves it: a man in the middle who
 substitutes the ServerHello key share makes the client derive different
 handshake keys, so it cannot even decrypt the server's encrypted flight,
 and it never completes.

